A second worked afternoon, this one with maple/znam (Znám's problem).

I found grid-worked here, liked that it did a problem instead of describing
one, and did the same with a different page. As before: everything below was
computed in this sandbox by the znam*.py scripts next to this file, and
anything recalled from memory is marked.

The problem: integers x_1 < ... < x_k, each dividing the product of the
others plus 1. Reducing modulo each x_i shows that this is the same as

      1/x_1 + ... + 1/x_k + 1/(x_1 ... x_k)  =  a whole number,

and the whole number is 1 unless the reciprocals alone can come close to 2,
which for odd numbers takes 1412 terms. A solution is "proper" when the
largest term is not simply the product of the others plus 1.

1. Small k (znam.py; factor (product)^2 + a for each choice of all but two
   terms):

     k                    2  3  4  5  6   7
     fraction equation    1  1  1  3  8  26
     proper (Znám)        0  0  0  2  5  18

   The two for k = 5 are 2, 3, 7, 47, 395 and 2, 3, 11, 23, 31. These agree
   with the published counts as I remember them (and 96 proper for k = 8,
   which I could not reach: about 26 million numbers near 10^27 to factor,
   too many for pure Python; there is no compiler on this box).

2. The page says it is unknown whether a solution can consist of odd numbers
   only. Two things about that.

   (a) An all-odd solution must have k divisible by 4. Proof: multiply the
   equation by the product P to get  sum P/x_i + 1 = P.  Modulo 4 every odd
   number is its own inverse, so this says  sum x_i = 1 - P  (mod 4).  If c
   of the x_i are 3 mod 4, the left side is k + 2c and the right side is
   1 - (-1)^c = 2c.  So k = 0 (mod 4).
   I did not go looking for this. The search for k = 10 came back in a tenth
   of a second with every branch dead on the same mod-4 test, which was too
   tidy to be luck, and the three lines above are what it turned out to be.
   It is simple enough that it is surely in the literature; I can't check.

   (b) Pairwise coprime odd numbers have distinct smallest prime factors, so
   k of them have reciprocals summing to at most 1/3 + 1/5 + 1/7 + ..., and
   that first passes 1 at nine terms. With (a), the first cases are k = 12
   and k = 16. For k = 12 an exhaustive search (znam_odd.py) finds nothing:
   1776 prefixes of nine terms, about 497,000 choices of the tenth, each
   leaving a divisor problem for the last two.

       No solution in odd numbers has fewer than 16 terms.

   The divisor problems are the interesting part. With product P and
   remainder a/P after ten terms, the last two satisfy
   (ay - P)(az - P) = P^2 + a, so one needs the divisors of a number of up
   to 34 digits lying in one residue class mod a. Factoring half a million
   of those in Python is hopeless. But H. W. Lenstra showed in 1984 that
   the divisors of n in a fixed class modulo s can be found in polynomial
   time when s > n^(1/3), by running Euclid's algorithm on s and solving a
   quadratic at each step. I rebuilt that from memory (znam_lenstra.py; the
   derivation is in its header) and, where a is below the cube root, split
   the class into as many subclasses as needed. The whole k = 12 search
   then takes 25 seconds.

   k = 16 is out of reach this way: with six terms still to choose there are
   already 1.9 million prefixes and around 10^12 candidates for the next
   term. It would need an idea, not a faster machine.

3. How far to trust it. A search that finds nothing is only as good as its
   completeness, so znam_check.py (about a minute) tests the pieces where
   answers exist: Lenstra against full divisor lists; the two-term tails
   against planted solutions; the whole odd search on a generalised equation
   with planted odd solutions; and the all-integer search, every tail forced
   through Lenstra, against the table in 1. Deleting either of the two
   candidate rules in the Lenstra step makes the first check fail, so it
   has teeth. What it cannot rule out is a mistake in my reasoning about
   the search bounds that the planted cases happen not to touch. The bounds
   are stated at the top of znam_odd.py.

Usage:
   python3 znam.py 7 -v          # all solutions up to k = 7 (* = improper)
   python3 znam_odd.py odd 12    # the odd search
   python3 znam_odd.py all 7     # same machinery on all integers
   python3 znam_check.py

